from pathlib import Path
import csv,html,subprocess,json
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];O=R/'publications';read=lambda p:list(csv.DictReader(p.open()));rows=read(R/'catalog.csv');alt=read(R/'conditional/results.csv');panels=read(R/'panels.csv');taxa=list(dict.fromkeys(x['target_taxon'] for x in panels));esc=html.escape
TITLE='MiPhyllo: taxon-stratified 16S and 28S PCR primer candidates for environmental DNA metabarcoding of Phyllodocida'
parts=[]
def page(s):parts.append('<section class="page">'+s+'</section>')
def table(headers,body):return '<table><thead><tr>'+''.join('<th>'+esc(x)+'</th>' for x in headers)+'</tr></thead><tbody>'+''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+esc(str(x))+'</td>' for x in row)+'</tr>' for row in body)+'</tbody></table>'
page(f'''<h1>{TITLE}</h1><p class="author">Fei Xia</p><p class="author">Computational preprint · 5 November 2026</p><h2>Abstract</h2><p>Taxonomic breadth and primer specificity can conflict when designing environmental DNA assays for diverse marine annelids. We integrated mitochondrial 16S and nuclear 28S designs across eight nested target units within Phyllodocida: the order, four suborders and three families. Frozen, species-deduplicated panels were searched for short, medium and long windows. Ordinary and degenerate counterparts shared binding endpoints, with degeneracy limited to eight variants per oligo. The catalogue comprises 94 current pairs and eight conditional alternatives. All 102 pairs were re-scanned using independent C++ and Python implementations with agreement in target and background outcomes. Family-level designs improved focal coverage: Nereididae 16S LD covered 58/61 references, while Hesionidae 28S SD covered 40/45. Order-wide coverage remained low. Latest Nereididae 28S SD covered 29/52 references with eight exact matches. A conditional Syllidae 16S S2D increased coverage from 110 to 126/180 and exact matches from seven to 48, but exhibited substantially greater loose-model background matching. These results support taxon-stratified candidate selection rather than a single universal assay. They represent computational matching, not experimentally measured amplification performance.</p><h2>1. Introduction</h2><p>Phyllodocida comprises divergent polychaete lineages for which a single short rRNA assay may not reconcile coverage and specificity. Scale-worm phylogenetic reference data provide an established starting point [1], while environmental DNA has been applied to marine metazoan surveys [2]. Broad-target primer designs such as MiFish illustrate the value of conserved binding regions surrounding variable sequence [3]; this study applies that general principle to annelid reference panels, without transferring fish-primer sequences or assuming equivalent performance.</p><p>We compare designs at the order, suborder and family levels, retain multiple amplicon lengths, and explicitly separate strict and loose background matching. MiPhyllo denotes the integrated Phyllodocida collection. The repository and current sequence identifiers use MiPhyllo; the original Aphroditiformia preprint remains a separate case study.</p>''')
page('''<h2>2. Materials and methods</h2><h3>2.1 Frozen references and taxonomic units</h3><p>Each gene panel retained one reference per named species, prioritizing sequence completeness during source curation. Target units were Phyllodocida; Aphroditiformia, Glyceriformia, Nereidiformia and Phyllodociformia; and Syllidae, Nereididae and Hesionidae. Panels overlap taxonomically and must not be summed as independent sampling. Direction-adjusted MAFFT alignments [4] guided homologous windows. Reference accessions, family assignments, alignments and frozen backgrounds are distributed with each unit.</p><h3>2.2 Candidate generation</h3><p>Current selected spans fall within S 100–200 bp, M 201–300 bp and L 301–480 bp. Earlier searches used narrower ranges, retained in source scripts. Latest expanded searches evaluated 18–30 nt oligos at 1 nt start increments, combining frequent haplotypes and consensus seeds, coverage-oriented and exact-oriented branches, spatially distributed windows and local refinement. Ordinary/D pairs share four binding endpoints; each oligo has degeneracy ≤8, restricted to its 3′ half. An ordinary sequence need not be nested within the D expansion.</p><p>Two concentration scenarios—50 nM per variant and 200 nM per direction—required minimum predicted Tm ≥45°C, F/R midpoint difference ≤5°C and predicted secondary-structure Tm &lt;55°C. Search procedures were staged heuristics, not exhaustive enumeration of all possible sequences.</p><h3>2.3 Matching and selection</h3><p>Target coverage required ≤2 mismatches per end, compatibility at each terminal three bases, total product length 100–500 bp and internal length ≥50 bp. Exact matching required zero mismatches at both ends, including IUPAC-compatible bases. All reference species remained in the denominator, including fragments lacking a complete window. Background scans allowed an internal gap of 0–1000 bp. Strict WC/GT used ≤2 mismatches and terminal compatibility; loose WC allowed ≤3 mismatches and loose GT ≤2 without terminal constraints. GT additionally permits primer-oriented G:A and T:C compatibility and is a sensitivity model.</p><p>The original Aphroditiformia study used a six-group polychaete background; Phyllodocida used an expanded marine background. Both retained absolute limits of ten strict and fifty loose hits per model. Later suborder/family designs required strict WC ≤1%, GT ≤3%, loose WC ≤5% and GT ≤10%, plus each strict close-relative count ≤max(2, floor(3% × close-relative N)). Marine screening was based on existing genus-level WoRMS assignments, not comprehensive species/locality verification.</p><p>Latest balanced selections did not reduce ordinary/D coverage or exact matching relative to preceding selections. Conditional alternatives retain strict and close-relative gates but remove loose-model hard vetoes. They are separate candidates, identified by 2 (e.g., S2/S2D), not two-pair mixtures.</p><h3>2.4 Independent verification</h3><p>All 102 selected pairs were freshly scanned on their own frozen panels, comparing C++ and independent Python outcomes record by record. Sequence exports, coordinates, degeneracy, species totals, bilingual tables and checksums were also checked. No wet-lab validation, common-panel comparison or independent holdout experiment is reported here.</p>''')
body=[]
for t in taxa:
 ps={x['gene']:x for x in panels if x['target_taxon']==t};body.append([t,ps['16S']['target_species'],ps['16S']['background_species'],ps['28S']['target_species'],ps['28S']['background_species']])
page('<h2>3. Results</h2><h3>3.1 Reference panels and coverage</h3>'+table(['Target unit','16S target','16S background','28S target','28S background'],body)+'''<p><b>Table 1.</b> Frozen reference species counts. Nested panels overlap; background definitions differ between design units.</p><img src="../figures/catalog_coverage.png"><p><b>Figure 1.</b> Current D coverage within each reference panel. S, M and L denote selected length classes. Missing Syllidae 28S L indicates no passing pair. Percentages are not common-panel performance comparisons.</p><p>Phyllodocida designs covered at most 71/515 16S and 53/302 28S references. Nereidiformia remained limited (62/315 and 45/133). Phyllodociformia reached 49/59 and 52/63, but its references represented Phyllodocidae only. Glyceriformia 16S SD/MD covered 14/20, with only one exact match each. Detailed ordinary/D outcomes and sequences for all units appear in Appendix A.</p>''')
page('''<h2>3.2 Family-level optimization and conditional alternatives</h2><p>Nereididae 16S LD covered 58/61 references, but its strict WC/GT background counts (30/58) exceeded those of SD (0/12), which covered 57/61. Latest 28S SD increased coverage from 28 to 29/52 while retaining eight exact matches; all four strict/loose background counts were zero. The 28S MD and LD retained low current coverage (11/52 and 4/52).</p><p>Syllidae current 16S SD, MD and LD covered 110, 104 and 87 of 180 references, respectively. No ordinary/D replacement improved or retained all four target metrics under the original complete background profile. Conditional S2D covered 126/180 with 48 exact matches, compared with 110 and seven for SD. However, strict WC/GT hits were 33/42 of 3,675 background references and loose hits were 1,007/601. Thus the apparent gain depends on how mismatch tolerance is treated. The conditional set is not merged into the current FASTA.</p><h2>4. Discussion</h2><p>The data favor stratified target design: broad order-level candidates show poor reference coverage, whereas several narrower panels produce higher within-panel coverage. This pattern does not establish superiority in a common sample or reference panel. Some gains reflect restricted target scope, different reference availability and a different near-relative background.</p><p>Degeneracy can increase compatible target diversity without increasing the number of primer pairs, but exact matching, background compatibility and the concentration of each variant remain distinct considerations. A high exact-match count does not rescue a low-coverage design, and low strict-background counts do not imply low loose-model matching. The conditional Syllidae designs make this tradeoff explicit.</p><p>Reference limitations include incomplete fragments, uneven taxonomic representation and inherited taxonomy. Lacydoniidae is absent from Glyceriformia references, Microphthalmidae from Nereidiformia references, and Phyllodociformia includes only Phyllodocidae. Syllidae 28S remains poorly covered. Neither all described species nor the full marine environmental community is represented. Primer-site matching also does not measure taxonomic resolution of the intervening amplicon.</p><h2>5. Conclusion and data availability</h2><p>MiPhyllo provides a traceable, taxon-stratified collection of 16S/28S candidates and explicit alternatives for eight Phyllodocida target units. Candidate use should consider target scope, exact matching and both background models. Frozen references, 94 current pairs, eight conditional pairs, per-species outcomes, source algorithms and independent re-scan records are available at <a href="https://github.com/GUIBA-EX/MiPhyllo">github.com/GUIBA-EX/MiPhyllo</a>. Full canonical identifiers follow MiPhyllo-{target}-{gene}-{length}[2][D]_{F|R}; these identifiers are consistent with the repository and manuscript name.</p><h2>References</h2><p>[1] Norlinder E et al. (2012). Phylogeny of scale-worms (Aphroditiformia, Annelida), assessed from 18SrRNA, 28SrRNA, 16SrRNA, mitochondrial cytochrome c oxidase subunit I (COI), and morphology. <i>Molecular Phylogenetics and Evolution</i>. PMID: 22789762.</p><p>[2] Environmental DNA metabarcoding for monitoring metazoan biodiversity in Antarctic nearshore ecosystems (2021). <i>PeerJ</i> 9:e12458. doi:10.7717/peerj.12458.</p><p>[3] Miya M et al. (2015). MiFish, a set of universal PCR primers for metabarcoding environmental DNA from fishes: detection of more than 230 subtropical marine species. <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 2:150088. doi:10.1098/rsos.150088.</p><p>[4] Katoh K, Standley DM (2013). MAFFT multiple sequence alignment software version 7: improvements in performance and usability. <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 30:772–780. doi:10.1093/molbev/mst010.</p>''')
for i,t in enumerate(taxa,1):
 rs=[x for x in rows if x['target_taxon']==t];s=f'<h2>Appendix A{i}. {t}</h2><p>Canonical prefix: MiPhyllo-{t}-. All sequences are 5′→3′. Covered and exact counts use the full target reference denominator. Strict and loose background values are WC/GT.</p>'
 s+=table(['Gene/pair','bp','Covered','Exact','Strict bg','Loose bg'],[[x['gene']+'-'+x['name'].split('-')[-1],x['span'],x['covered']+'/'+x['target_species'],x['exact']+'/'+x['target_species'],x['WC']+'/'+x['GT'],x['WCloose']+'/'+x['GTloose']] for x in rs])
 s+='<h3>Primer sequences</h3>'+table(['Gene/pair','Forward','Reverse'],[[x['gene']+'-'+x['name'].split('-')[-1],x['F'],x['R']] for x in rs])
 s+='<p>Binding coordinates, accession identifiers and per-species matches: panels/'+t+'/results.csv and species.csv in the repository. Ordinary/D pairs have identical endpoints within each design.</p>';page(s)
page('<h2>Appendix B. Conditional alternatives</h2><p>Suffix 2 identifies a distinct alternative, not a mixture. Strict and close-relative gates are retained; loose-model counts are diagnostic rather than selection vetoes.</p>'+table(['Target/gene/pair','Covered','Exact','Strict bg','Loose bg'],[[x['target_taxon']+' '+x['gene']+' '+x['name'].split('-')[-1],x['covered']+'/'+x['target_species'],x['exact']+'/'+x['target_species'],x['WC']+'/'+x['GT'],x['WCloose']+'/'+x['GTloose']] for x in alt])+'<h3>Sequences (5′→3′)</h3>'+table(['Pair','Forward','Reverse'],[[x['target_taxon']+' '+x['name'].split('-')[-1],x['F'],x['R']] for x in alt])+'<p>Full names and coordinates are in conditional/results.csv; all eight pairs were independently re-scanned. Copyright © 2026 Fei Xia. Repository copyright and third-party data terms apply.</p>')
css='''@page{size:A4;margin:16mm 16mm 17mm;@bottom-right{content:counter(page);font:8pt Arial}}body{font:10pt/1.35 Arial,sans-serif;color:#182c36}h1{font-size:22pt;line-height:1.15}h2{font-size:15pt}h3{font-size:11pt}p{margin:7pt 0}.author{text-align:center}.page{break-before:page}.page:first-child{break-before:auto}h2,h3{break-after:avoid}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:7.8pt;line-height:1.25;margin:9pt 0}th,td{padding:4pt 3pt;border-bottom:1px solid #ccd8dd;text-align:left}th{background:#e7f0f3}tr{break-inside:avoid}img{width:100%}a{color:#18566c}'''
(O/'MiPhyllo_preprint.html').write_text('<!doctype html><meta charset="utf-8"><title>'+TITLE+'</title><style>'+css+'</style>'+''.join(parts))
subprocess.run(['pandoc',str(O/'MiPhyllo_preprint.html'),'-f','html','-t','gfm','-o',str(O/'MiPhyllo_preprint.md')],check=True)
(O/'MiPhyllo_manifest.json').write_text(json.dumps(dict(title=TITLE,author='Fei Xia',current_pairs=len(rows),conditional_pairs=len(alt),units=taxa,source_commit='fd29c4b17f7a97c8ce06e6dec8e052bf9d8e0d4c'),indent=2))
print('Built manuscript',len(parts),'sections/pages')
